@@s003 30 points in 2D, stretched along the diagonal: the two coordinates are correlated.
@@s004 Step 1: the mean μ = (5.00, 2.89). PCA describes the variations around it.
@@s005 Steps 2–4: the covariance and its eigenvectors. <b>PC1</b> = (0.83, 0.55) with λ₁ = 3.51; <b>PC2</b> = (−0.55, 0.83) with λ₂ = 0.29. The arrows are 2√λ long: the spread along each.
@@s006 PC1 alone explains 3.51 / (3.51 + 0.29) = <b>92.3%</b> of the variance. One keeps only it: k = 1.
@@s007 Each point is projected onto PC1: a = PC1ᵀ(x − μ), <b>a single number</b> instead of two. The dashed line is the information lost.
@@s008 The reconstruction x̂ = μ + a · PC1 puts every point on the line. It loses only the variance along PC2 (7.7%).
