@@s012 Eigenvalues and PCA
@@s013 Lectures 11–12 and the exercises of week 12, including <b>Assignment 2</b> (<i>PCA for shape generation</i>). PCA looks for a new <a href="w06-basi.html">basis</a> in which the data are described with few numbers: the directions in which they vary most. The <a href="w09-covarianza-correlazione.html">covariance</a> and the eigenvectors are needed. Exam question <b>Q10</b>. DW chapters 18–20.
@@s014 Eigenvalues and eigenvectors
@@s015 A v = λ v
@@s016 An <b>eigenvector</b> v is a direction that the matrix A does not rotate: it only stretches or shortens it, by the factor λ (the <b>eigenvalue</b>).
@@s017 C = [[2, 1], [1, 2]]: λ = <mark class="due">3</mark> with v = (1, 1)/√2; λ = <mark class="due">1</mark> with v = (−1, 1)/√2
@@s018 Check: C · (1, 1) = (3, 3) = 3 · (1, 1). Verified with <code>np.linalg.eigh</code>.
@@s019 Example of DW 19: the diagonal stays still (λ = 1), the perpendicular direction is flipped (λ = −1). The determinant is the product of the eigenvalues. A rotation has no real eigenvectors (complex eigenvalues).
@@s020 <mark class="ok">symmetric</mark> matrix (like the covariance): real eigenvalues, <mark class="ok">orthogonal</mark> eigenvectors
@@s021 That is why the eigenvectors of a covariance form an orthonormal basis: the axes of the ellipse of the data.
@@s022 Eigenvalue = variance along the eigenvector
@@s023 The variance of the data in the direction of a unit vector v is <span class="m">vᵀCv</span>. If v is an eigenvector of C: vᵀCv = λ vᵀv = <b>λ</b>. With C = [[2, 1], [1, 2]] and v = (1, 1)/√2: vᵀCv = 3 (verified). So λᵢ = σᵢ² and σᵢ = √λᵢ, as the assignment recalls.
@@s024 Directions that the matrix does not rotate
@@s025 PCA in five steps
@@s026 From the data to the principal components
@@s027 1. <mark class="dato">μ</mark> = mean of the rows; Xc = X − μ
@@s028 The data are centred: PCA describes the variations around the mean.
@@s029 Covariance matrix d × d (DW divides by N; with N − 1 the eigenvalues change by a factor, not the eigenvectors).
@@s030 3. eigenvectors and eigenvalues of C, 4. sorted by decreasing λ
@@s031 The first eigenvector is the direction of maximum variance, the second the maximum among those perpendicular to the first, and so on.
@@s032 The new basis, reduced. k is chosen with the cumulative variance (below).
@@s033 PCA on a cloud of 30 points
@@s034 Mean, eigenvalues and eigenvectors computed with NumPy (<code>eigh</code>, covariance with 1/N).
@@s035 There and back
@@s036 Coordinates in the <b>latent space</b>: inner products with the eigenvectors (orthonormal basis ⇒ no inverse needed).
@@s037 Reconstruction in the data space. With k &lt; d it is an approximation: the variance of the discarded directions is lost.
@@s038 The implementation (verified on the faces)
@@s039 Mean per column, centred data, covariance (146 × 146 for the faces).
@@s040 <code>eigh</code> is for symmetric matrices: it returns increasing eigenvalues and eigenvectors <b>as columns</b>. <code>[::-1]</code> reverses the order: from the largest.
@@s041 One keeps the first k columns. With x as a row, (x − μ) @ Φ is equivalent to Φᵀ(x − μ).
@@s042 Assignment 2: the faces
@@s043 120 faces, each with 73 points (x, y) ⇒ 146 features. Numbers obtained with the course data (<code>face_shape_data('./db/')</code>):
@@s044 k components
@@s045 explained variance (cumulative)
@@s046 With k = 5 (those of the assignment) the first face is reconstructed with an RMS error of 0.0024, against a scale of the data of about 0.09. The rank of the centred data is 119 = N − 1: with 120 faces there are at most 119 components with non-zero variance.
@@s047 PCA as a generative model
@@s048 The mean face ("base face", Tasks 3–4).
@@s049 Moving along a single component one sees what it represents (width of the face, opening of the mouth, rotation…). σᵢ = √λᵢ: for the first three 0.056, 0.049, 0.031. Staying within ±3σ keeps the faces "plausible".
@@s050 Task 6: a line in the latent space becomes a gradual transformation between two faces.
@@s051 Task 9: with huge values the reconstruction is still a linear combination of shapes, but it no longer looks like a face.
@@s052 Q10
@@s053 (a) bases and transformations: the new basis Φ, orthonormal, and the change of coordinates. (b) dimensionality reduction and the assignment: cumulative variance, choice of k, reconstruction. (c) generative model: mean face, ±3σ, interpolation. (d) eigenvalues, covariance and basis: λ = variance along the eigenvector, symmetry ⇒ orthogonal eigenvectors.
@@s054 Cumulative variance and face generation
@@s055 The curse of dimensionality
@@s056 With many features the space behaves in a counter-intuitive way: it is the <b>curse of dimensionality</b>, one of the goals of week 12. Three effects, all measurable with a few lines of NumPy.
@@s057 <b>1 · The data become sparse.</b> To cover an interval with 10 points, 10 are enough; a square with the same density needs 100, a cube 1000, a space of 10 dimensions 10¹⁰. With a fixed number of examples, more features means points ever more isolated.
@@s058 <b>2 · The volume escapes to the corners.</b> The sphere inscribed in the cube [−1, 1]ᵈ occupies 78.5% of the cube in 2D, 52% in 3D and only 0.25% in 10D. Almost all the points are far from the centre, near the corners.
@@s059 <b>3 · All distances look alike.</b> Taking 500 random points in the cube [0, 1]ᵈ, the ratio between (maximum distance − minimum distance) and minimum distance from a point is about 29 in 2D, 2.5 in 10D, 0.41 in 100D and 0.11 in 1000D. The "nearest neighbour" is not much nearer than the others: methods based on distances, such as k-NN, lose their meaning.
@@s060 The remedy is that of this week: <b>reduce the dimensions</b> before measuring distances, with PCA (the first components keep almost all the variance, like the 5 components out of 146 of the faces) or by choosing a few good features.
@@s061 In high dimension distances look alike
